      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.104,
            0.418,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "SOME APPLICATIONS OF TRIGONOMETRY"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.862,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "139"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.147,
            0.135,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.327,
            0.147,
            0.501,
            0.166
          ],
          "content": "DB  = (40 + x)  m",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.173,
            0.577,
            0.192
          ],
          "content": "Now, we have two right triangles ABC and ABD."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.212,
            0.205,
            0.231
          ],
          "content": "In   ABC,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.294,
            0.199,
            0.442,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": "\\tan 6 0 ^ { \\circ } = { \\frac { \\mathrm { A B } } { \\mathrm { B C } } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.262,
            0.128,
            0.277
          ],
          "content": "or,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.328,
            0.247,
            0.898,
            0.287
          ],
          "content": "\\left[ \\sqrt{3} \\right. = \\left. \\frac{h}{x} \\right.\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.304,
            0.206,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": "In   ABD,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.294,
            0.291,
            0.442,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": "\\tan 3 0 ^ { \\circ } = { \\frac { \\mathrm { A B } } { \\mathrm { B D } } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.353,
            0.139,
            0.371
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.325,
            0.338,
            0.898,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": "\\left[ { \\frac { 1 } { \\sqrt { 3 } } } \\; = \\; { \\frac { h } { x   +   4 0 } } \\right]\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.395,
            0.283,
            0.415
          ],
          "content": "From (1), we have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.348,
            0.391,
            0.448,
            0.415
          ],
          "content": "h = { \\stackrel { \\cdot } { x } } { \\sqrt { 3 } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.426,
            0.754,
            0.454
          ],
          "content": "Putting this value in (2), we get $\\left( x \\sqrt { 3 } \\right) \\sqrt { 3 } = x + 4 0 , \\mathrm { i . e . } , 3 x = x + 4 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.466,
            0.14,
            0.485
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.349,
            0.466,
            0.425,
            0.485
          ],
          "content": "x = \\: 2 0",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.501,
            0.135,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.348,
            0.496,
            0.899,
            0.521
          ],
          "content": "h = 20\\sqrt{3}\\tag{[From (1)]}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.528,
            0.538,
            0.549
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, the height of the tower is $\\overline { { 2 0 } } \\sqrt { 3 }$ m."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.565,
            0.902,
            0.585
          ],
          "content": "**Example 6 :** The angles of depression of the top and the bottom of an 8 m tall building"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.583,
            0.558,
            0.662
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "from the top of a multi-storeyed building are $3 0 ^ { \\circ }$ and $4 5 ^ { \\circ }$ , respectively. Find the height of the multi-storeyed building and the distance between the two buildings."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.667,
            0.558,
            0.764
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** In Fig. 9.9, PC denotes the multi-storyed building and AB denotes the 8 m tall building. We are interested to determine the height of the multi-storeyed building, i.e., PC and the distance between the two buildings, i.e., AC."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.586,
            0.619,
            0.902,
            0.812
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_6_image_24.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.764,
            0.557,
            0.881
          ],
          "content": "Look at the figure carefully. Observe that PB is a transversal to the parallel lines PQ and BD. Therefore, $\\angle  QPB$ and $\\angle \\mathrm { P B D }$ are alternate angles, and so are equal. $\\mathrm{So} \\angle \\mathrm{PBD} = 30^{\\circ}$ . Similarly, $\\angle  PAC = 45^{\\circ}$ In right   PBD, we have"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.704,
            0.821,
            0.791,
            0.841
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 9.9"
        },
        {
